join Lebo (Neutral) left
join Hope (Neutral) right
join Amos (Neutral) left
Lebo: The Nkosi boy went Thursday.
Amos: Mm.
Lebo: I nearly went. Did you know that?
Amos: I knew.
Lebo: Ninety-nine. There was work in Polokwane, proper work, a man's brother-in-law wanted someone who could weld.
Lebo: I had a bag packed for eleven days.
Amos: Twelve.
Lebo: ...Twelve.
- Why did you stay?
	Hope: Why did you stay?
	Lebo: I couldn't work out who'd fix things.
	Lebo: That's not noble. I want to be clear it's not noble. It was mostly that I couldn't picture the place without me in it, and that's vanity more than anything.
Amos: It wasn't vanity.
Lebo: ...No. All right.
Lebo: I'm glad the boy went, though. He should go. There was never anything here that was going to hold him and there shouldn't have been.
Lebo: There was something here that held me. Turns out that's different.
